1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,470
I am the Time Chronicler! No matter which country you live in today, you obey laws, pay taxes, and submit to the authority of a government or supreme leader.

2
00:00:16,470 --> 00:00:28,620
Yet have you ever wondered: just a few thousand years ago, there were no kings, no crowns, and no concept of a government anywhere on Earth?

3
00:00:28,620 --> 00:00:37,970
For ninety-five percent of Homo sapiens' three-hundred-thousand-year history, humans lived in fiercely equal nomadic hunter-gatherer bands.

4
00:00:37,970 --> 00:00:46,680
In that ancient world, there were no police forces, no courts, and no single human held the right to boss around another.

5
00:00:46,680 --> 00:00:54,590
If an arrogant hunter stood up, pounded his chest, and screamed: 'I am the boss of this tribe!'—do you know what happened?

6
00:00:54,590 --> 00:01:02,500
The entire tribe would gather around him in a tight circle and burst out in unanimous, humiliating laughter!

7
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:10,610
Anthropologists call this the 'Reverse Dominance Hierarchy'—where the weak united together to crush any would-be tyrant before he could rise.

8
00:01:10,610 --> 00:01:23,680
So how did that world of perfect equality shatter, paving the way for absolute monarchs ruling over millions of subservient subjects?

9
00:01:23,680 --> 00:01:36,670
To unlock this mystery, we must travel to the Kalahari desert in southern Africa, where prehistoric equality survived until modern times.

10
00:01:36,670 --> 00:01:45,220
Among the Ju/'hoansi Bushmen of the Kalahari, an incredible tradition persisted for centuries—known as 'Insulting the Meat'.

11
00:01:45,220 --> 00:01:55,170
If an elite hunter took down a massive giraffe all by himself, instead of cheering, the tribe would mockingly spit and laugh in his face!

12
00:01:55,170 --> 00:02:03,000
They would jeer: 'Where did you drag this skinny bag of bones from? This looks worse than a diseased, dead goat!'

13
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:12,270
In 1969, when anthropologist Richard Lee gifted them a massive twelve-hundred-pound black ox, the tribe pulled the exact same trick on him!

14
00:02:12,270 --> 00:02:22,300
When a baffled Lee asked why, a village elder explained: 'When a young man kills much meat, his heart swells, and he imagines himself a chief.'

15
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:29,610
'And tolerating pride means that one day, that unchecked arrogance will lead to human blood being spilled!'

16
00:02:29,610 --> 00:02:39,360
Prehistoric foragers understood that absolute power was a deadly virus; if allowed to spread, it would poison egalitarian human freedom.

17
00:02:39,360 --> 00:02:48,070
If an individual persisted in demanding submission despite warnings, the entire tribe banded together and banished him into the wilderness!

18
00:02:48,070 --> 00:02:55,100
Yet this ironclad egalitarian defense was permanently shattered roughly twelve thousand years ago.

19
00:02:55,100 --> 00:03:04,930
Glaciers receded, global climate transformed, and humanity stumbled upon the ultimate catalyst of inequality—agriculture!

20
00:03:04,930 --> 00:03:18,320
Most people assume agriculture was a glorious, voluntary blessing—yet documented archaeology reveals a very dark reality!

21
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:27,630
In the Middle East's Fertile Crescent, humans planted wild emmer wheat and barley, trapping themselves in permanent mudbrick settlements.

22
00:03:27,630 --> 00:03:36,260
As hunter-gatherers, humans worked just three to four hours a day, spending the rest of their time resting, storytelling, and playing.

23
00:03:36,260 --> 00:03:45,250
Yet the moment humans became farmers, life degraded into dawn-to-dusk backbreaking toil under the scorching sun and heavy rain!

24
00:03:45,250 --> 00:03:52,920
Yet a far more dangerous psychological revolution was brewing inside the human relationship with food storage.

25
00:03:52,920 --> 00:04:00,190
Hunted meat spoiled within forty-eight hours, meaning sharing was the only rational economic choice.

26
00:04:00,190 --> 00:04:07,380
Dried grain and barley, on the other hand, could be stockpiled inside sealed clay granaries for years without rotting!

27
00:04:07,380 --> 00:04:16,090
And thus private property was born—where one family's silo overflowed while the neighboring hut's children went hungry!

28
00:04:16,090 --> 00:04:24,640
The moment grain stockpiles grew, predatory nomadic raiders began eyeing the defenseless farming villages from the distant hills.

29
00:04:24,640 --> 00:04:34,350
A farmer slaved for nine grueling months, only for horse-mounted bandits to sweep in overnight, torch the huts, and loot every last bushel!

30
00:04:34,350 --> 00:04:42,820
Farmers faced an existential crisis: you cannot reap wheat with a sickle while simultaneously wielding a heavy copper spear!

31
00:04:42,820 --> 00:04:50,850
In sheer desperation, the terrified villagers knelt before the strongest, most violent warrior in their valley.

32
00:04:50,850 --> 00:04:56,400
'Please protect us! Defend our granaries, and we will reward you with whatever you demand!'

33
00:04:56,400 --> 00:05:03,710
The hulking warlord grinned—and the most dangerous, irreversible contract in human history was drawn!

34
00:05:03,710 --> 00:05:13,060
The warrior declared: 'The terms are simple: hand over twenty percent of every single harvest to feed me and my armed militia!'

35
00:05:13,060 --> 00:05:20,650
Thus began history's very first protection racket—what modern civilized societies politely call taxation!

36
00:05:20,650 --> 00:05:27,840
The warlord's gang successfully drove off the bandits, but once the threat passed, they never went back to farming.

37
00:05:27,840 --> 00:05:36,070
They realized that brandishing weapons to consume other people's labor was infinitely more comfortable than sweating in the dirt!

38
00:05:36,070 --> 00:05:44,740
They erected high mudbrick fortifications around the central granary, gradually reducing the villagers into their personal servants.

39
00:05:44,740 --> 00:05:51,570
Whenever a farmer refused to pay the tax, the very spears hired to protect him were pointed straight at his throat!

40
00:05:51,570 --> 00:06:00,400
In the ancient Sumerian language of Mesopotamia, these dominant military warlords were called 'Lugal'—literally meaning 'Big Man'.

41
00:06:00,400 --> 00:06:09,630
The Lugal bellowed: 'Every grain of this soil belongs to me, and your very survival depends upon my mercy!'

42
00:06:09,630 --> 00:06:17,940
Yet ruling thousands of resentful farmers purely through raw physical terror was an exhausting, unstable gamble.

43
00:06:17,940 --> 00:06:26,650
Soldiers grew tired, copper weapons broke, and a nighttime peasant uprising could reduce the warlord's compound to ashes.

44
00:06:26,650 --> 00:06:34,800
The warlord realized that shackling human minds was a thousand times more permanent than chaining their wrists in iron!

45
00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:43,950
To achieve this masterstroke, he struck a historic unholy alliance with the most revered faction in society—the temple priests!

46
00:06:43,950 --> 00:06:54,860
Above the Mesopotamian mudbrick skylines of Uruk, Eridu, and Ur rose colossal Ziggurats—towering stepped temple pyramids.

47
00:06:54,860 --> 00:07:04,170
High priests climbed the sacred heights and preached: 'This city does not belong to mortals; the river and the crops are owned by the sky gods!'

48
00:07:04,170 --> 00:07:14,040
'And the gods themselves have anointed our Lugal as their steward on Earth—disobeying him is an unforgivable sin against heaven itself!'

49
00:07:14,040 --> 00:07:23,390
In a single brilliant stroke, a brutal extortion racket was miraculously baptized into sacred divine will!

50
00:07:23,390 --> 00:07:32,020
Preserved on ancient cuneiform prisms, the 'Sumerian King List' officially documents the name of the first recognized king in history.

51
00:07:32,020 --> 00:07:40,330
The inscription reads: 'When kingship descended from heaven, kingship was in Eridu. In Eridu, Alulim became king!'

52
00:07:40,330 --> 00:07:49,040
A heavy horned stone helmet was placed upon the king's head—the direct prehistoric ancestor of the modern golden crown.

53
00:07:49,040 --> 00:07:56,630
Yet the ultimate totalitarian leap was engineered by Mesopotamia's first great conqueror—Sargon of Akkad!

54
00:07:56,630 --> 00:08:03,820
Rising from a humble royal cupbearer in the twenty-fourth century BCE, Sargon ruthlessly seized power.

55
00:08:03,820 --> 00:08:13,330
He conquered thirty-four rival city-states, creating history's first permanent professional standing army of fifty-four hundred soldiers!

56
00:08:13,330 --> 00:08:23,040
Ancient chronicles record that every single day, this massive military force ate bread directly from Sargon's imperial table!

57
00:08:23,040 --> 00:08:32,510
And when soldiers depend entirely on the king for their daily bread, their loyalty belongs to the monarch alone—never to the people!

58
00:08:32,510 --> 00:08:41,980
Meanwhile along the Nile, King Narmer unified Egypt, declaring himself not merely a prophet, but a literal living god incarnate!

59
00:08:41,980 --> 00:08:50,490
The famous Narmer Palette depicts the pharaoh single-handedly smashing the heads of his kneeling enemies with a colossal stone mace!

60
00:08:50,490 --> 00:08:59,240
To carve eternal submission into the human psyche, monarchs erected towering pyramids and colossal stone monuments.

61
00:08:59,240 --> 00:09:08,510
When a humble peasant looked up at a mountain of stone built for one dead man, he forgot that the king was merely mortal flesh and blood!

62
00:09:08,510 --> 00:09:18,980
Hereditary monarchy was locked in—where even an incompetent or tyrannical child inherited absolute dominion over millions solely through royal blood.

63
00:09:18,980 --> 00:09:28,570
Intoxicated by this god-complex, some emperors grew so delusional that they genuinely believed they could command the sun and tides!

64
00:09:28,570 --> 00:09:38,800
Ancient Babylonian tyrant Nimrod claimed absolute sovereignty over life and death, only to succumb to the most humiliating end in dust!

65
00:09:38,800 --> 00:09:51,670
The Pharaoh of Egypt enslaved entire generations in his supreme hubris, yet his golden chariots were swept into watery oblivion beneath the surging Red Sea!

66
00:09:51,670 --> 00:10:01,580
Renowned historian Ibn Khaldun observed in his 'Muqaddimah' that royal dynasties rise on the raw power of 'Asabiyyah'—collective social solidarity.

67
00:10:01,580 --> 00:10:11,530
Yet once supreme power is won, the rulers sink into corrupt decadence, and within three generations, the mighty dynasty crumbles like a house of cards.

68
00:10:11,530 --> 00:10:21,280
Ancient monarchs believed their reign would endure forever; today, their withered remains lie silent beneath museum glass display cases.

69
00:10:21,280 --> 00:10:34,870
We may have abandoned gold crowns, yet is our modern world truly free from the legacy of that ancient Mesopotamian grain guardian?

70
00:10:34,870 --> 00:10:49,180
Every police department, tax revenue service, courthouse, and border checkpoint in your country traces back to that ancient grain protection pact!

71
00:10:49,180 --> 00:10:58,250
Humanity struck a profound psychological bargain: trading the fierce freedom of the wilderness for the artificial security of civilization.

72
00:10:58,250 --> 00:11:07,880
In exchange for that physical protection, humans surrendered their autonomy to codes of law, minted currency, standing armies, and governments.

73
00:11:07,880 --> 00:11:17,670
Yet history repeatedly cements one undeniable truth: any ruler's authority remains legitimate only so long as it upholds justice and human dignity!

74
00:11:17,670 --> 00:11:26,980
Whenever a king or leader elevated himself above the people, his empire inevitably choked upon its own suffocating pride.

75
00:11:26,980 --> 00:11:41,050
The Kalahari foragers lacked writing systems and built no palaces—yet they mastered a profound wisdom that modern civilization so often forgets.

76
00:11:41,050 --> 00:11:54,440
The truth that no human is born to be owned by another, and no mortal forehead was engineered to bow in terror before another man.

77
00:11:54,440 --> 00:12:08,350
If an ancient Sumerian king walked into your city today, he would likely trade his horned stone crown for a bespoke suit and run for office!

78
00:12:08,350 --> 00:12:22,980
Do you believe humanity truly required central rulers, or was that ancient egalitarian wilderness superior? Share your verdict in the comments below!

79
00:12:22,980 --> 00:12:36,090
To explore more hidden truths and wild ironies across the centuries, subscribe right now to Simple History Teller! See you in the next episode!
